{
 "id": "L3",
 "n": 3,
 "title": "Food and drink",
 "goal": "Order food and drinks, count things and people, and say what you like to eat.",
 "notes": [
  {
   "h": "Asking for things",
   "p": "Say the thing first and put {{주세요}} (\"please give me\") last. Adding {{좀}} before {{주세요}} makes the request softer, like \"could I have some\". To ask for the bill, say {{계산해 주세요}}.",
   "ex": [
    [
     "물 좀 주세요",
     "Some water, please."
    ],
    [
     "이거 주세요",
     "This one, please."
    ],
    [
     "메뉴 좀 주세요",
     "The menu, please."
    ]
   ]
  },
  {
   "h": "Counting with native numbers",
   "p": "To count, say the thing, then a native Korean number, then a counter word: {{커피 두 잔}} is \"coffee, two, cups\". Right before a counter, four numbers get shorter: {{하나}} becomes {{한}}, {{둘}} becomes {{두}}, {{셋}} becomes {{세}}, {{넷}} becomes {{네}}. Common counters: {{개}} for things, {{명}} for people, {{잔}} for cups and glasses, {{병}} for bottles. Sino-Korean numbers never go here: {{삼 잔}} is wrong. Staff ask how many you are with the respectful {{몇 분이세요?}}, but you answer about yourselves with {{명}}.",
   "ex": [
    [
     "커피 두 잔 주세요",
     "Two coffees, please."
    ],
    [
     "맥주 세 병 주세요",
     "Three bottles of beer, please."
    ],
    [
     "두 명이에요",
     "There are two of us."
    ],
    [
     "이거 한 개 주세요",
     "One of these, please."
    ]
   ]
  },
  {
   "h": "Eating, drinking and \"I'll have\"",
   "p": "The thing you eat or drink is marked with {{을}} after a consonant and {{를}} after a vowel: {{밥을}}, {{커피를}}. In casual speech it is often dropped, but never swap the two. Polite verbs end in {{요}}: {{먹어요}} (eat), {{마셔요}} (drink). To say what you'll have, or to ask what someone would like, add {{-ㄹ래요}} after a vowel and {{-을래요}} after a consonant: {{마실래요}}, {{먹을래요}}. {{뭐}} means \"what\".",
   "ex": [
    [
     "커피를 마셔요",
     "I drink coffee."
    ],
    [
     "밥을 먹어요",
     "I eat rice."
    ],
    [
     "뭐 먹을래요?",
     "What would you like to eat?"
    ],
    [
     "저는 맥주 마실래요",
     "I'll have a beer."
    ]
   ]
  },
  {
   "h": "Have, don't have, and \"not\"",
   "p": "{{있어요}} means \"there is / I have\" and {{없어요}} means \"there isn't / I don't have\". They are a pair, so never say {{안 있어요}}. {{맛있어요}} (delicious) is literally \"taste exists\" and {{맛없어요}} is \"no taste\". For other verbs, put {{안}} right before the verb to mean \"not\": {{안 먹어요}}, {{안 매워요}}.",
   "ex": [
    [
     "소고기 있어요?",
     "Do you have beef?"
    ],
    [
     "죄송합니다. 소고기는 없어요.",
     "Sorry, we don't have beef."
    ],
    [
     "저는 고기를 안 먹어요",
     "I don't eat meat."
    ]
   ]
  },
  {
   "h": "Sounds: linking and hidden changes",
   "p": "A final consonant slides into a following vowel: {{먹어요}} is said {{머거요}} and {{맛있어요}} is said {{마시써요}}. After a final k, t or p sound, the next plain consonant becomes tense: {{맥주}} sounds like {{맥쭈}}, {{숟가락}} like {{숟까락}}. When a syllable ends in two consonant letters, only one is said: {{닭고기}} is {{닥꼬기}}. A p sound before m turns into m: {{밥 먹어요}} sounds like {{밤 머거요}}.",
   "ex": [
    [
     "맛있어요",
     "It's delicious."
    ],
    [
     "맥주 두 병 주세요",
     "Two bottles of beer, please."
    ],
    [
     "닭고기 있어요?",
     "Do you have chicken?"
    ]
   ]
  }
 ],
